*** Requirements
Python 3 (developed and verified against 3.13)
http://www.python.org/

Tornado Web Server 6.x
http://www.tornadoweb.org/

SQLAlchemy 2.x
http://www.sqlalchemy.org/

**** Install requirements in a virtualenv

  python3 -m venv .venv
  .venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt

*** Running

Before running the first time, to set up necessary files run:

  .venv/bin/python setup.py

This writes secrets.py and generates a self-signed localhost.crt /
localhost.key pair. Then edit secrets.py to add the missing fields
(client_id and client_secret are only needed for Google sign-in; the game
itself runs fine with them left empty).

Then, to run locally for development:

  .venv/bin/python tau.py --debug --port=8000 --ssl_port=8001 \
      --certfile=localhost.crt --keyfile=localhost.key

and open https://localhost:8001/ (note: https, and the ssl_port). Because
localhost.crt is self-signed, the browser shows a certificate warning the
first time; choose Advanced -> Proceed to accept it.

http://localhost:8000/ also works: the HTTP port serves nothing but a
redirect to HTTPS on the ssl_port. Note that http://localhost:8001/ does
not work, and logs an 'SSL Error ... http request' warning, because 8001
speaks TLS only.

--certfile/--keyfile default to the production chained.pem/domain.key, so
deployments do not need to pass them. Pass them explicitly when running
locally, since the production certificate is issued for taugame.com (and
expired in 2020).

To run the tests:

  .venv/bin/python test_fast_board.py

*** Releasing

Docker Desktop is not required. Colima provides the docker daemon from a small
Linux VM, and release.sh drives it for you. One-time setup:

  brew install colima docker docker-buildx

("docker" here is the CLI only, not Desktop.) Homebrew's buildx needs to be
registered with the docker CLI, by adding this to ~/.docker/config.json:

  "cliPluginsExtraDirs": ["/opt/homebrew/lib/docker/cli-plugins"]

buildx is required because this Mac is arm64 while the GCE instances are
x86_64, so the image has to be cross-built for linux/amd64. An arm64 image
pushed to GCR starts and dies immediately with "exec format error", the backend
never becomes healthy, and the load balancer serves 502 for the whole site.
release.sh builds with --platform linux/amd64 and then refuses to go any
further unless the built image really reports amd64, so this cannot reach
production silently. The smoke test then runs that same amd64 image under qemu
emulation, which colima provides; it is a little slower to start but it
exercises the artefact that actually ships.


Docker also needs its own credentials for the push. "gcloud auth login" only
authenticates the gcloud CLI, so this is a separate one-time step:

  gcloud auth configure-docker

Without it the push fails with "Unauthenticated request ... does not have
permission artifactregistry.repositories.uploadArtifacts", even though gcloud
itself is logged in. release.sh checks for this before building and tells you
to run the above.

There are three modes:

  ./release.sh              build, smoke test, push to GCR, cycle the instance group
  ./release.sh --dry_run    build and smoke test only; pushes nothing
  ./release.sh --serve      build, smoke test, then leave it running for you to
                            poke at; Ctrl-C stops it and cleans up

The smoke test runs in all three modes, so a container that does not build or
does not serve is never pushed. It mirrors production in the two ways that have
actually bitten:

- It starts the container with the command the instance template uses,
  "python ./tau.py" with no arguments, rather than the Dockerfile ENTRYPOINT.
  That means no --debug and the argparse defaults of port 80 and ssl_port 443,
  which is what really runs in production.

- It runs against a throwaway MySQL container rather than SQLite, because
  production uses Cloud SQL MySQL and SQLite is far more permissive. The
  container is pinned to MySQL 5.7's default sql_mode, which includes
  ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY; that setting rejects some aggregate queries SQLite
  accepts happily, so a leaderboard page can be perfectly fine locally and
  return 500 in production.

A couple of scores and states are seeded before the checks run. Without rows in
the database the leaderboard queries short-circuit and never execute, so the
aggregate that ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY polices would go untested.

It then sets a name, walks the main pages, every leaderboard view, a graph and
a recap, creates a game, opens the game page, and checks the container log for
tracebacks.

--dry_run never pushes and never touches the running instance, so it is safe at
any time and is the quickest way to find out whether a change breaks the
container. --serve is the same but leaves the container up at
https://localhost:8401/ for manual checks; the certificate is self-signed, so
accept the browser warning once, and use the https URL directly because the
container only knows its own internal port and its http->https redirect will
not point back at the mapped port.

All three modes start colima if it is not already running and stop it again on
the way out, including when the build fails. If colima was already running
before the script started, it is left running afterwards, so it stays out of
the way of an existing session. The smoke test binds ports 8400 and 8401 rather
than 8000 and 8001, so a development server can keep running throughout.

Useful colima commands: "colima status", "colima stop", "colima delete" to
remove the VM, and "brew uninstall colima docker" to remove the tools.

**** How the deploy reaches the running server

The service runs as the zonal managed instance group tau-game-ig in
us-central1-a, from instance template tau-game-ig-4, which declares the
container as gcr.io/tau-game/websockettau:latest. Because the tag never
changes, pushing a new image does not by itself change what is running; the
group has to be cycled so the VMs pull again. release.sh does that with:

  gcloud compute instance-groups managed rolling-action replace tau-game-ig \
    --project=tau-game --zone=us-central1-a --max-unavailable=1

"replace" deletes each VM and creates a fresh one from the template, so the
docker cache is cold and the :latest tag is genuinely re-pulled. The lighter
alternative, and the direct equivalent of the old "gcloud compute instances
reset", is:

  gcloud compute instance-groups managed rolling-action restart tau-game-ig \
    --project=tau-game --zone=us-central1-a --max-unavailable=1

That reboots in place and keeps the boot disk, which is faster but can reuse a
cached image carrying the same tag. Prefer replace unless the extra boot time
matters.

The group holds a single instance, so --max-unavailable=1 means a short gap
while the replacement boots. For no gap, use --max-surge=1 --max-unavailable=0
instead, which brings the new VM up before removing the old one at the cost of
briefly running two.

Note that replace gives the new VM new internal and external IP addresses. That
is fine behind the load balancer, which tracks the group rather than an address.

**** Building without any local container runtime

The image can instead be built entirely in GCP using the already-installed
gcloud, with no VM on the Mac at all:

  gcloud builds submit --tag gcr.io/tau-game/websockettau --project=tau-game

That uploads the build context, builds in Cloud Build and pushes the result to
GCR in one step, replacing the build and push halves of release.sh. It needs
the Cloud Build API enabled on the project. Note that it pushes, so there is no
equivalent of --local.

.dockerignore keeps the uploaded context to about 10MB either way; without it
the .venv and .git directories push it past 50MB.

To build and run with Docker (experimental):

  docker build -t websockettau . && docker run --rm -p 8000:8000 -p 8001:8001 websockettau

To avoid having to rebuild the docker container on every change, you can run from your work directory:

  docker run --rm -p 8000:8000 -p 8001:8001 -v $PWD:/live -w /live websockettau

To run against a test version of a mysql db:

  docker run --rm -p 8000:8000 -p 8001:8001 -v $PWD:/live -w /live -e db=mysql -e mysql_password=$MYSQL_PASSWORD -e mysql_addr=$LOCAL_IP_FROM_IFCONFIG:3306 -e db_name=tau_game_2 websockettau python ./tau.py --debug --port=8000

Connect to actual mysql for testing by running the proxy:

  curl -o cloud_sql_proxy https://dl.google.com/cloudsql/cloud_sql_proxy.darwin.amd64
  ./cloud_sql_proxy -instances=tau-game:us-central1:tau-game-db=tcp:0.0.0.0:3306

Root access is required to bind to port 80 which is the default, so using ports 8000 and 8001 avoids this. The --debug flag allows the use of ^C to kill the server (very handy for restarting it during development).

For other options, type:

  .venv/bin/python tau.py --help

*** Adding a new game type (as of July 15, 2012)
- Implement the rules in game.py.
- Add a name mapping to static/game_list.js.
- Add a name mapping and URL mapping to tau.py.
- Add a name mapping to constants.py.
- Add a rules description and name mapping to templates/about.html.
- Add an entry to each dict in preset_decks.py, otherwise --preset crashes
  on the new type.
- (Sometimes applicable) Add changes to playing area in static/tau.js.
- (If adding new cards) Make the following updates:
    static/tau.js: getImgClass(), get_card_number()
    static/styles.css: new class similar to .regularTau, .projectiveTau, etc.

How to set up Fedora 16 server:
$ ssh root@...
$ passwd # to set password to something sane
$ yum install git
$ yum install python
$ yum install gcc-c++
$ git clone https://github.com/jasharpe/websockettau.git
# install python dependencies
$ cd ~/websockettau && python3 -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt
# create secrets.py
# Get google client id and secret from https://console.developers.google.com/project/apps~tau-game/apiui/credential
$ cd ~/websockettau && cat > secrets.py
cookie_secret = "SOME STRING HERE"
client_id = "GOOGLE CLIENT ID"
client_secret = "GOOGLE CLIENT SECRET"
# Set up certificates by creating localhost.crt certificate, and put in websockettau directory.
# Open ports, add to /etc/sysconfig/iptables:
# -A RH-Firewall-1-INPUT -m state --state NEW -m tcp -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT
# -A RH-Firewall-1-INPUT -m state --state NEW -m tcp -p tcp --dport 443 -j ACCEPT
$ ./restart.sh

# Set up ntp, so that time stays up to date (otherwise backups will fail when the clock skew gets too high!)
$ yum install ntp
$ ntpd

# setup automatic backups to S3
$ yum install s3cmd
$ s3cmd --configure
# follow the prompts...
# test that backups will work properly
$ ./run_backup
# verify that the backup appears in S3
# set up automatic backups. s3_crontab is a template. Modify it to suit you.
$ crontab s3_crontab

### How to set up GCP instance

1. Create GCP project.

2. Upload Docker image to GCR (called `gcr.io/$PROJECT/websockettau`). Probably requires enabling the GCR API. Normally this is just `./release.sh`, which also restarts the instance; the underlying steps are:

```
docker build -t gcr.io/tau-game/websockettau .
docker push gcr.io/tau-game/websockettau
```

Or, without a local container runtime:

```
gcloud builds submit --tag gcr.io/tau-game/websockettau --project=tau-game
```

3. Enable Stackdriver Logging API.

4. Create a GCP instance that runs the container uploaded (or do via the UI), and populates the environment variables needed by db.py.
